auto_explain_z
==============

auto_explain_z is a standalone executor hook module for writing sampled
execution plans to separate binary files. It intentionally does not use
PostgreSQL's regular log path for plan payloads.

Load it with session_preload_libraries, shared_preload_libraries, or LOAD:

    LOAD 'auto_explain_z';
    SET auto_explain_z.log_min_duration = 0;

Build and install it with PGXS:

    make PG_CONFIG=/path/to/pg_config
    make install PG_CONFIG=/path/to/pg_config

The PGXS install includes extension metadata, so CREATE EXTENSION
auto_explain_z can load the module in the current session. For automatic plan
logging in new sessions, continue to use session_preload_libraries or
shared_preload_libraries.

Run TAP tests with PostgreSQL's installed PGXS harness when the target
PostgreSQL build has TAP support enabled:

    make installcheck PG_CONFIG=/path/to/pg_config

For builds without --enable-tap-tests, the standalone Makefile provides a
direct TAP runner. Point PG_TEST_PERL_DIR at the PostgreSQL source tree that
matches the target server:

    make tapcheck PG_CONFIG=/path/to/pg_config \
        PG_TEST_PERL_DIR=/path/to/postgresql/src/test/perl

tapcheck automatically includes Perl modules installed below ~/perl5. If
dependencies such as IPC::Run are installed elsewhere, pass PERL5LIB as usual.
Use make fulltapcheck with the same variables to run every decoder node case
through every supported format and EXPLAIN mode.

Binary files are written per backend to auto_explain_z.directory, which
defaults to auto_explain_z below the data directory. The "simple" profile is a
compact binary plan tree with costs, optional execution stats, optional
buffer/WAL counters, query identity, and optional query text. The "full"
profile adds binary-coded EXPLAIN detail fields such as output expressions,
quals, join/hash/merge conditions, scan/index/TID conditions, sort/group/hash
keys, and rows-removed counters.
FDW, direct foreign modify, foreign modify, and custom scan EXPLAIN callbacks
are captured as text side-channel details, because PostgreSQL exposes those
extension points as arbitrary formatter output rather than typed plan fields.

The format is versioned at the file level and record-oriented:

* file header: magic, format version, file compression method, PostgreSQL
  version, backend start time, backend start epoch, and backend pid
* file body: one compressed stream when compression is enabled; individual
  records are not compressed independently
* record header: compact size-control bytes, implicit record number,
  millisecond timestamp delta from backend start, profile, query flags,
  optional query id, duration, and payload length
* payload: compact log context, template metadata, and either a recursive full
  plan tree or a recursive per-execution metrics tree, followed by query-level
  details

Internal records and payloads do not carry per-record magic numbers or format
versions. Small integer fields use size-control bits and are stored as 1, 2,
4, or 8 bytes as needed.

The payload log context preserves the useful parts of regular log-line
metadata without writing to the regular log path: backend type, database
OID/name, effective user OID/name, authenticated user name, application name,
and client host/port when present. Timestamp and pid live in the compact
record/file headers. Format v6 stores the first full context in each file and
lets later records reference it while it remains unchanged.
Format v7 adds compact node bits for PostgreSQL's Disabled flag and stores
index scan direction only for index scan nodes. Format v8 stores a Plan
Identifier on every record, using the structural plan shape hash, so records
can be grouped by plan without first resolving template references.

Template compression is controlled by:

* auto_explain_z.template_cache: enable template definitions and references
* auto_explain_z.template_fast_path: after a query id has a template, allow
  simple metric-free references without hashing the whole plan every time; the
  cached plan pointer is part of the fast-path key
* auto_explain_z.template_fast_path_recheck: executions between safety rechecks
  of the query-id fast path; default 0 disables periodic rechecks
* auto_explain_z.max_templates: cap unique templates admitted to the cache
* auto_explain_z.template_min_plan_bytes: only template plans above this size
  (default 0, so repeated small plan shapes can be templated)
* auto_explain_z.template_omit_query_text: store query text on template
  definition records, but omit it from later template-reference records

When auto_explain_z is loaded through shared_preload_libraries, template
admission is shared across backends under auto_explain_z.max_templates. Each
binary file still writes its own template definition before writing references,
so decoding never depends on templates stored in another backend's file. If the
module is loaded later with LOAD or session_preload_libraries, it falls back to
the same per-file behavior with a backend-local admission cache.

Templates are keyed by query id plus a structural plan-shape hash. The hash
includes relation/index identity, node type hierarchy, relevant plan options,
and full-profile expression trees where those would affect reconstructed
EXPLAIN output. The per-file definition dictionary resets when a backend
rotates to a new binary file, so every template reference is resolvable from
records in the same file.
Template definition records store template_id, plan identifier, optional query
text, query id, and the full static plan tree. Template-reference records store
template_id, plan identifier, and optional per-execution sections; only query id
is recovered from the template definition when omitted from the ref record.
When runtime metrics are present, refs also carry a metrics-only tree in the
same node order, and the decoder overlays it on the cached static tree. Simple
profile records without ANALYZE omit that metrics tree entirely, so repeated
executions do not repeat relation names, index names, quals, sort/group/hash
keys, estimates, or other static EXPLAIN details.

Retention cleanup is opportunistic and runs when a backend opens or rotates a
binary log file:

* auto_explain_z.retention_max_files: maximum matching .aez files to keep
* auto_explain_z.retention_max_size: maximum total matching .aez bytes to keep
* auto_explain_z.retention_cleanup_interval: minimum time between scans

Retention is disabled by default. Cleanup only considers files in
auto_explain_z.directory whose names match auto_explain_z.file_prefix and end
in .aez, and it does not remove the file currently open by the backend doing
the cleanup.

Writes are batched per backend before they enter the compression/file-write
path. auto_explain_z.pending_buffer_size controls this pending buffer in KiB
and defaults to 1024. Set it to 0 to disable pending buffering and flush every
record immediately.

Decode logs with auto_explain_z_dump:

    auto_explain_z_dump --format text path/to/file.aez
    auto_explain_z_dump --format json path/to/file.aez
    auto_explain_z_dump --format yaml path/to/file.aez
    auto_explain_z_dump --format xml path/to/file.aez

The decoder resolves template-reference records using prior template
definition records from the same file. lz4 and zstd files require either the
corresponding Python module or an available command-line decompressor.
By default, decoded output is normalized to PostgreSQL-style EXPLAIN fields.
Use --raw to inspect the AEZ record headers, log context, template metadata,
and other diagnostic fields preserved in the binary stream.

Benchmark auto_explain_z against contrib/auto_explain with pgbench:

    auto_explain_z_bench --postgres-source /path/to/postgresql --build-contrib \
        --scale 10 --clients 8 --duration 30 \
        --iterations 3 --workloads select-only,simple-update,tpcb-like

    auto_explain_z_bench --scale 3 --clients 4 --duration 2 \
        --workloads shape-simple-point,shape-simple-range,shape-join-agg,shape-complex,shape-very-complex \
        --auto-explain-formats text,json,yaml,xml \
        --aez-profiles simple,full --compressions none,lz4,zstd \
        --template-modes on,off

The standard comparison matrix is available as a shortcut:

    auto_explain_z_bench --standard-matrix --scale 10 --clients 8 \
        --duration 30 --iterations 3

Separate benchmark entry points are also installed for the common comparison
axes. All of them accept the normal auto_explain_z_bench options for scale,
clients, duration, iterations, pg_config, JSON output, and server paths:

    auto_explain_z_bench_all_modes
    auto_explain_z_bench_templates
    auto_explain_z_bench_profiles
    auto_explain_z_bench_compression
    auto_explain_z_bench_shapes
    auto_explain_z_bench_formats

auto_explain_z_bench_all_modes runs the broad matrix: simple/complex/very
complex/node-suite workloads, auto_explain text/json, auto_explain_z
simple/full, compression none/lz4/zstd, and template cache on/off.
auto_explain_z_bench_templates isolates template cache on/off.
auto_explain_z_bench_profiles isolates simple versus full auto_explain_z
profiles. auto_explain_z_bench_compression isolates none/lz4/zstd.
auto_explain_z_bench_shapes isolates plan complexity across the built-in
shape workloads. auto_explain_z_bench_formats compares regular auto_explain
text/json/yaml/xml against auto_explain_z simple/full.

The benchmark creates a temporary cluster, initializes pgbench, restarts the
server for baseline, auto_explain, and auto_explain_z scenarios, and reports
mean pgbench throughput/latency, throughput standard deviation, sampled server
CPU percentage, regular-log bytes, binary-log bytes, and plan-log bytes per
transaction. By default it uses pgbench's select-only workload and compares
regular text auto_explain output against the simple auto_explain_z profile.
When --postgres-source is provided, the benchmark can use source-tree binaries
and build/load contrib/auto_explain from that tree while loading auto_explain_z
from this standalone checkout.
Built-in pgbench workloads are select-only, simple-update, and tpcb-like. The
shape-* workloads create additional indexed lookup, range aggregate, join,
complex CTE/aggregate, very complex CTE/window/join plans, and a broad
shape-node-suite workload.
Use --aez-profiles, --workloads, --iterations, --analyze, --buffers,
--compression, --compressions, --template-cache, --template-modes,
--auto-explain-format, and --auto-explain-formats to compare other logging
modes and matrices.

Compression defaults to lz4 when PostgreSQL is built with lz4 support, and to
none otherwise. zstd is available when PostgreSQL is built with zstd support.

Current scope
-------------

This module is deliberately separate from contrib/auto_explain. It installs
its own executor hooks, owns the auto_explain_z GUC namespace, and writes plan
payloads only to its binary files.

The simple profile is intended as the low-overhead baseline. The full profile
duplicates the core EXPLAIN walk for common plan details while keeping the
on-disk representation binary-first. It emits the common core runtime side
channels: trigger and JIT summaries, sort and incremental sort stats, hash and
hash aggregate stats, memoize stats, tuplestore storage stats, index search
counts, bitmap heap block counts, ModifyTable conflict/MERGE counters, and
runtime subplan pruning counts. MERGE action summaries are emitted as static
details. FDW/custom EXPLAIN callback output is included as compressed text
details; callback failures during logging are captured as text details instead
of aborting the completed query.
